Subject: add a /run boot tier to the duktape rules.d search
Source: downstream patch for polkit 124
Upstream: not submitted; needs agreement on the PACKAGE_RUN_DIR tier and on
  staying silent when that tier is absent

The duktape backend (src/polkitbackend/polkitbackendduktapeauthority.c,
built when -Djs_engine=duktape is set) compiled exactly two rules
directories: PACKAGE_SYSCONF_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d" and
PACKAGE_DATA_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d". That gives an administrator tier
under /etc and a vendor tier under /usr/share, with no tier for
transient, boot-scoped authorization policy under /run.

polkit_backend_common_js_authority_constructed() in that file allocates
rules_dirs with g_new0 (gchar *, 3) and fills index 0 and 1, leaving
index 2 as the NULL terminator load_scripts() and setup_file_monitors()
both use to end their walk. This patch grows the allocation to 4 entries,
inserts PACKAGE_RUN_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d" at index 1 between the
existing two, and leaves index 3 as the new NULL terminator that g_new0
already zero-initializes. PACKAGE_RUN_DIR is a new compiled-in macro
defined as the literal "/run": unlike sysconfdir and datadir, this
project's meson.build (meson_version: '>= 0.50.0') has no runstatedir
builtin option to derive it from (Meson never gained a project-level
"runstatedir" builtin; only 'localstatedir', 'sysconfdir', and the other
long-standing directory options exist), and /run is not an administrator
choice on Nex the way /etc is, so the other Nex boot-tier repairs in this
plan (krb5's /run/krb5, libinput's /run/libinput) also compile it in as
a literal rather than adding a build option for it.

load_scripts() sorts every basename it finds across all three
directories with polkit_backend_common_rules_file_name_cmp()
(src/polkitbackend/polkitbackendcommon.c), which is unchanged by this
patch. That function compares basenames first and, on a tie, falls back
to comparing full paths with the comment "/etc wins over /usr". A
byte-wise comparison of "/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/NAME" against
"/run/polkit-1/rules.d/NAME" and "/usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/NAME"
already sorts "/etc" ahead of "/run" ahead of "/usr" ('e' 0x65 < 'r'
0x72 < 'u' 0x75), so inserting the /run entry between the existing two
in rules_dirs is enough on its own to give the administrator tier
precedence over the new boot tier, and the boot tier precedence over the
vendor tier, for a same-named rules file, without touching the
comparator. This model is additive rather than masking: every file from
every directory is loaded and executed, and only a same-named file's
runtime rule callback precedence is decided by this sort order, so a
uniquely named file from any tier still runs regardless of what exists
in the other tiers.

load_scripts() also logged "Error opening rules directory: ..." through
polkit_backend_authority_log() (which writes to both syslog and stdout)
every time it could not open a configured directory, with no distinction
between a real failure and a directory that simply does not exist yet.
/etc and /usr/share are normally present already, so this rarely fired
in practice, but /run/polkit-1/rules.d is not created by this package
and has no other populator on Nex; polkit_backend_common_reload_scripts()
calls load_scripts() again on every rules-directory change signalled by
setup_file_monitors(), so an absent /run tier would otherwise log this
message on every reload for the lifetime of the daemon. This patch keeps
g_dir_open()'s failure silent when the error is G_FILE_ERROR_NOENT and
still logs any other failure, such as a permissions problem, exactly as
before.

--- a/src/polkitbackend/meson.build
+++ b/src/polkitbackend/meson.build
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
   '-D_POLKIT_BACKEND_COMPILATION',
   '-DPACKAGE_DATA_DIR="@0@"'.format(pk_prefix / pk_datadir),
   '-DPACKAGE_SYSCONF_DIR="@0@"'.format(pk_prefix / pk_sysconfdir),
+  '-DPACKAGE_RUN_DIR="/run"',
   '-D_XOPEN_SOURCE=700',
   '-D_GNU_SOURCE=1',
 ]
--- a/src/polkitbackend/polkitbackendduktapeauthority.c
+++ b/src/polkitbackend/polkitbackendduktapeauthority.c
@@ -123,9 +123,16 @@
                         &error);
       if (dir == NULL)
         {
-          polkit_backend_authority_log (POLKIT_BACKEND_AUTHORITY (authority),
-                                        "Error opening rules directory: %s (%s, %d)",
-                                        error->message, g_quark_to_string (error->domain), error->code);
+          /* A missing rules directory is normal for an optional tier such as
+           * PACKAGE_RUN_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d", which nothing populates
+           * until an administrator or an early-boot helper creates it. Stay
+           * silent for that one, expected case so a reload does not log the
+           * same non-error on every rules directory change; still log any
+           * other failure, such as a permissions problem. */
+          if (!g_error_matches (error, G_FILE_ERROR, G_FILE_ERROR_NOENT))
+            polkit_backend_authority_log (POLKIT_BACKEND_AUTHORITY (authority),
+                                          "Error opening rules directory: %s (%s, %d)",
+                                          error->message, g_quark_to_string (error->domain), error->code);
           g_clear_error (&error);
         }
       else
@@ -245,9 +252,17 @@

   if (authority->priv->rules_dirs == NULL)
     {
-      authority->priv->rules_dirs = g_new0 (gchar *, 3);
+      /* Administrator, boot, then vendor: the array holds 3 directory
+       * entries plus the NULL terminator load_scripts() and
+       * setup_file_monitors() walk it by. polkit_backend_common_rules_file_name_cmp()
+       * sorts same-named rules by full path, and "/etc" < "/run" < "/usr"
+       * lexically, so listing the tiers in this order also gives the
+       * administrator tier precedence over the boot tier, and the boot tier
+       * precedence over the vendor tier, for a same-named rules file. */
+      authority->priv->rules_dirs = g_new0 (gchar *, 4);
       authority->priv->rules_dirs[0] = g_strdup (PACKAGE_SYSCONF_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d");
-      authority->priv->rules_dirs[1] = g_strdup (PACKAGE_DATA_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d");
+      authority->priv->rules_dirs[1] = g_strdup (PACKAGE_RUN_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d");
+      authority->priv->rules_dirs[2] = g_strdup (PACKAGE_DATA_DIR "/polkit-1/rules.d");
     }

   setup_file_monitors (authority);
